course: dictionary
lesson: 3811
title: Words of the water's edge
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe a place, and you can work out their meaning by picturing it. «Set me down on any of their quays or foreshores, and I am home again.» A quay is a stone landing-place for ships, so a foreshore must be somewhere by the water where boats and people meet.
explain: The tide decides what a foreshore is like. When the tide is low, the sea goes out and the foreshore is uncovered. When the tide is high, the sea comes back in and covers it. Ask: what word would fit here? A beach or a river bank could fit, so the foreshore is the land along the edge of the sea, lake or river.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The children played on the foreshore while the tide was low.» Put in "the land along the water's edge" and the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. If it does not make sense, read on and look for clues.
fact: The foreshore is the part of a beach between the high-tide line and the low-tide line.
fact: The sea covers the foreshore at high tide and leaves it uncovered at low tide.
fact: Rock pools are often found on the foreshore, where small sea creatures are left behind when the tide goes out.
fact: The foreshore is also the land along the edge of a sea, lake or river.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, «foreshore» is also used for parks and walkways along the water.
exercise: «Set me down on any of their quays or foreshores, and I am home again.» What is a foreshore in this sentence? | A bag for carrying fish | A tall tower in the middle of a town | *A place along the water's edge, where boats and people meet | A kind of boat with sails
exercise: The foreshore is covered by the sea at high tide. When is it uncovered? | Only when it rains | Only in winter | At high tide | *At low tide
exercise: In Australia, a sign says «Foreshore walk, this way». What might a foreshore be here? | A road for cars only | *A walkway along the water | A shop selling ice cream | A cave under a cliff
test: Which part of a beach is the foreshore? | The sea itself, far out from the shore | The dry sand at the top of the beach, far from the sea | The cliffs behind the beach | *The part between the high-tide line and the low-tide line
test: Why might you find a rock pool on the foreshore? | Because the sea never reaches the rocks | Because rock pools are built by people | *Because the tide goes out and leaves water behind in the rocks | Because rocks grow in the sand
exam: Why can the foreshore be dangerous if the tide comes in quickly? | Because the sea is always still there | Because the sand is too hot to walk on | Because there is nothing to see | *Because the sea can cover it fast and you may get cut off
